<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">National Party (South Africa)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1914">1914</a>–<a href="/years/1997">1997</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The National Party was a South African political party founded in 1914 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/j-b-m-hertzog">J. B. M. Hertzog</a> to represent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaner</a> nationalism. After Hertzog's fusion with Smuts in 1934, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/d-f-malan">D. F. Malan</a> led a breakaway Purified National Party, which became the Reunited National Party and won the 1948 election. It governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> until 1994 and built the system of apartheid, which it negotiated away under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/f-w-de-klerk">F. W. de Klerk</a>; renamed the New National Party, it dissolved in 2005.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The National Party was founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bloemfontein">Bloemfontein</a> in 1914 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaner</a> nationalists soon after the Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> was established. Its founding was rooted in disagreements between Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-botha">Louis Botha</a> and his first Minister of Justice, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/j-b-m-hertzog">J. B. M. Hertzog</a>, whom Botha removed from the cabinet after Hertzog spoke publicly against the government's &quot;one-stream&quot; policy in 1912. Hertzog and his followers in the Orange Free State advocated a &quot;two-stream&quot; policy of equal rights for the English and Afrikaner communities. Nationalists in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a> and Cape soon followed, and three provincial organisations existed in time for the 1915 general elections.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The party first governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> in 1924. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a> it merged with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-african-party">South African Party</a>, and a splinter faction, the Purified National Party, became the official opposition. The Reunited National Party, formed after the fragmentation of the war years, came to power in 1948 and revived the name National Party, forming the first all-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaner</a> cabinet since 1910. After the Commonwealth condemned apartheid, its government made South Africa a republic outside the Commonwealth. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-general-assembly">United Nations General Assembly</a> labelled apartheid a crime against humanity on 16 December 1966.</p><p>In 1958 the party took 55% of the vote and its first clear majority, and in 1977 it had its best result, 64.8% of white voters and 134 of 165 seats. From the 1960s it divided between verkramptes, who opposed any departure from rigid apartheid, and verligtes, who were more moderate. Under P. W. Botha in the 1980s it created separate chambers for Coloureds and Indians but refused meaningful political rights to Black South Africans. F. W. de Klerk succeeded Botha in 1989 and persuaded the party to negotiate an end to apartheid. In 1994 it won 20.39% of the vote and 82 seats, and in 1997 it became the New National Party.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The party was a loose federation of four provincial parties, Cape, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a>, Natal and the Orange Free State, each with its own leader, press and constitution, linked by a Federal Council. The leader, who was also prime minister and later State President, was elected by the parliamentary caucus. A political culture rooted in Calvinist tradition, with an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaner</a> habit of obedience towards leaders, meant that under Botha decisions moved from the bottom up to the top down. A new party called National Party South Africa was registered on 5 August 2008, with no formal connection to the earlier one.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “National Party (South Africa)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…leaders remained prominent: Steyn was revered as an elder statesman, Christiaan de Wet was a leader of the rebellion of 1914, and J. B. M. Hertzog, a judge and general of the republic, founded the <b>National Party</b> in 1914 and became prime minister of South Africa in 1924.</blockquote><a href="/empire/orange-free-state">Orange Free State · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>A republic was the long-standing goal of the Afrikaner nationalist <b>National Party</b>, in power since 1948, and of its prime minister Hendrik Verwoerd, who won a referendum of white voters on 5 October 1960 with about 52 per cent of the vote.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-south-africa">Republic of South Africa · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>J. B. M. Hertzog, who founded the <b>National Party</b> in 1914, governed from 1924 in a pact with the Labour Party, made Afrikaans an official language in 1925 and won recognition of dominion equality in the Balfour Declaration of 1926, confirmed by the Statute of Westminster in 1931…</blockquote><a href="/empire/union-of-south-africa">Union of South Africa · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In the election of May 1948 the <b>National Party</b> of D. F. Malan, speaking for Afrikaner nationalism and for white fears of a growing Black urban population, won a majority of seats, though not of votes, on the programme of apartheid, “separateness”.</blockquote><a href="/movement/anti-apartheid-movement">Anti-apartheid movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1948 the <b>National Party</b> governed South Africa, and the white minority held all political power under the system of apartheid.</blockquote><a href="/songs/asimbonanga">Asimbonanga</a></li><li><blockquote>The Afrikaner <b>National Party</b> had come to power in 1948 and governed for the next 46 years; under apartheid, a brutal system of racial segregation whose name meant &quot;separateness&quot; in Afrikaans, the white minority held all political power and black South Africans were forced to live in poverty-stricken…</blockquote><a href="/songs/meadowlands">Meadowlands</a></li></ul></section>
